
- Implement a hint system.

  Requirements:

	* Keep track of the number of hints used for each
          country->country, Countrycard and the entire game for
          scoreboard purposes.
	* Hint one country in a CountryCard at a time.
	* Use a progressive system:
	  "Saudi Arabia": "..."  -> "----- ------" -> "S---- ------" -> "S---- ------a"
	* When a country is fully hinted, further hinting should
          deobfuscate the next country.
	* Solved countries can't be hinted.
	* Don't increment the hint tally if no more countries can be
          hinted any further
	* Hints should be requestable through hotkey, e.g. Ctrl+H.
  
  Implementation:

	Implement a Country-class. The class is going to be able to
	represent both unguessed countries and solved countries
	through a print method.

	The class should take country data as an initial argument, and
	make use of the "flag" and "name" attributes when constructing
	a country string.

	The class should have settable "hint" and "solved" parameters.

	When the "solved" parameter is True, the method should print
	the entire name unobfuscated.

	When the "solved" parametere is False, the method should print
	an obfuscated country name according to the requirements section.

	When requesting further hinting, the class should report
	whether the hinting was successfully incremented. This allows
	using a for loop to loop through available class instances,
	attempting to increment hints for each one. Only increment the
	game hint tally if a class instance reported success, then end
	the loop.

 	Can build a single markdown for the country list by building a
  	list over class instances, potentially sorted by some rule,
  	then calling a print method on all of them in a list
  	comprehension, then concatenating the results.

  Considerations:

  	Is it better to store these methods in the graph itself? Some
  	kind of broader CountryClass in place of the current graph
  	nodes?  Had thought it best to keep graph-related concerns
  	(neighbors, edges) to the graph and game-related concerns
  	(guesses, hints) to the App subclass, but there is already
  	some bleed (number of guesses is stored in the graph, the App
  	is clumsily making sure all CountryCards are kept up to
  	date). Storing more information/methods in the graph would
  	make things more unified maybe.

	If we store guesses in the graph node, the hint level for a
	bordering country in one CountryCard would affect the same
	country in other CountryCards. Unless we somehow store guess
	state per source-country, which might require two edges per
	border (inbound and outbound). Inbound/outbound might be
	desirable regardless if we e.g. want to make a flashcard-style
	game. Adding an additional edge set would require reworking
	some graph logic, and we'd have to provide some parameter so
	we could choose whether the guess_borders should be two-way or
	one-way.

	Guess order, which could be a desirable field to sort on, is
	currently stored in the graph itself. If we don't store these
	methods in the graph we'd have to sort the BorderingCountry
	class instances according to the guess order pulled from the
	graph.

	Guess order is global guess order, which works fine when
	sorting done countries locally in a CountryCard. If we
	implement inbound/outbound edges we can store guess order in
	the edge itself, allowing us to store local guess order.

	Moving towards storing more things in the graph could
	potentially allow the graph itself to notify subscribers about
	changes, allowing more scoped messaging than we currently
	have. Would be neat if each CountryCard could manage their own
	subscription to their CountryNode.

	To keep things flexible, we should probably subclass the
	igraph node somehow, extending it with our game-related
	methods. That should enable us to keep our graph-logic mostly
	intact while allowing the game to run more game-centric
	methods on the nodes.

	Instead of hinting each country to completion to the next one,
	we could hint all countries to the next hint level before
	starting over. This would probably be frustrating for a user
	that is only stuck on one particular country.

	The hint levels are heterogeneous (esp. if we include the
	flag), meaning that a total hint tally in itself doesn't say
	much. Might be therefore be interesting to break hints down by
	hint level as well. Hints by level could be tallied up by
	inspecting all HintClasses, but that would require somehow
	keeping them around in some form until scoreboard-time.
  
- In the country-list in each CountryCard, use non-breaking spaces
  within each country name (and the space) between the flag and the
  country name.

- Implement a random start

- Review "independent" variable; Uzbekistan is set to False.

- Exclude Dominican Republic and Haiti, unless some better solution
  can be found.

- Improve message posting. Currently, all CountryCards are notified
  whenever there's some sort of change to the graph, which leads to a
  lot of unneccessary recomposes.
